<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<TcPlcObject Version="1.1.0.1">
  <POU Name="F_InitTestBP" Id="{872b1e54-dad7-464f-b50c-ec80c2c884b3}" SpecialFunc="None">
    <Declaration><![CDATA[(*
    F_InitTestBP - establishes a BP set for testing, i.e. a BP set whose status
    bits are all in an OK state.

    PURPOSE
        Give a test a single place to obtain an ST_BeamParams that describes
        "the beam is fine": the beam parameters handed in are returned
        unchanged and every status-bearing member is forced to its non-faulting
        value. A test then overrides only the members its assertion is about.

    USAGE
        stBP : ST_BeamParams;
        stBP := F_InitTestBP(stBP);
        The function is stateless and reads no global state, so it may be called
        from any test without side effects. Status members the test needs to be
        bad may still be overridden by the caller afterwards.

    OBJECTIVES
        - One definition of a healthy test BP, so tests cannot drift apart on
          what "OK status" means.
        - Absorb additions to ST_BeamParams in a single place.

    HISTORY
        2020-11-18 / Alex Wallace
            Function added with an empty body in "Tests for the margin logic"
            (commit 16e0988): BP was never read and the result was never
            assigned, so a caller got the ST_BeamParams field defaults.
        2026-11-28 / Scirix Agent
            Implemented the documented status initialisation - result := BP,
            xValid TRUE, per-attenuator xAttOK good, per-aperture xOK TRUE
            (audit finding #1062).
*)
FUNCTION F_InitTestBP : ST_BeamParams
VAR_INPUT
    BP : ST_BeamParams; // Basis BP set, a complete ST_BeamParams: nTran = pre-optic
                        // transmission, 0..1 of full transmission; nRate = pulse rate
                        // in Hz; neVRange = photon-energy range bit mask; nBCRange =
                        // beam-class range bit mask; astAttenuators = per-attenuator
                        // transmission in %; astApertures = Width/Height in mm. Only
                        // its status members are overwritten, with the OK values
                        // documented in the implementation.
END_VAR
VAR
    stResult : ST_BeamParams; // Working copy of BP with the status bits set to OK
    idx : UINT; // Loop index over the attenuator and aperture status arrays
END_VAR
]]></Declaration>
    <Implementation>
      <ST><![CDATA[// Keep the requested beam parameters, then force every status bit to OK.
stResult := BP;

// BP set is valid (readback) / acknowledged (request).
stResult.xValid := TRUE;
// xValidToggle is left as it was passed in: it is the interface watchdog toggle,
// not an OK/NOK status, so neither of its values is "good" or "bad".

FOR idx := 1 TO PMPS_GVL.AUX_ATTENUATORS DO
    // Cleared first, so a stale fault carried in by the caller cannot survive:
    // the result is exactly the good status word F_SetGoodAttStatus builds, the
    // one F_AttenuatorOK accepts for an attenuator in PMPS mode.
    stResult.astAttenuators[idx].xAttOK := F_SetGoodAttStatus(0);
END_FOR

FOR idx := 1 TO PMPS_GVL.MAX_APERTURES DO
    // Aperture OK: no error and not in motion.
    stResult.astApertures[idx].xOK := TRUE;
END_FOR

F_InitTestBP := stResult;]]></ST>
    </Implementation>
  </POU>
</TcPlcObject>